Subject: [PATCH] VideoWorker: fix T23 VPU teardown order on in-process rebuild
Target: prudynt-t @ f4b32289359d176fb0795d9f3735af0bd9d6d2fd (branch stable)

On Ingenic T23 (isp_bypass=true), an in-process video rebuild (global_restart_video,
triggered by a stream-config change such as stream mode / bitrate / fps) froze the
stream and wedged the VPU until a full reboot.

Root cause: the worker teardown disabled the framesource (IMP_FrameSource_DisableChn)
while it was still IMP_System_Bind-ed to the just-stopped encoder. DisableChn then
blocks ~6s waiting for a downstream consumer that cannot drain, and leaves the VPU
faulted (hwicodec: vpu channel run failed / i264e_slice_write failed). The old prudynt
(3c8e835) used the same teardown code, but its runtime/buffer state let DisableChn
drain quickly; f4b3228's changes exposed the latent ordering bug.

Fix: tear down in SDK-correct reverse-of-create order -- deinit() (which unbinds
fs->osd->enc and destroys the encoder channel) and delete the encoder FIRST, then
disable the now-unbound framesource. With no consumer to wait for, DisableChn returns
immediately and the rebuild completes cleanly. Encoder and framesource are checked
independently (each is created independently). No behaviour change on non-T23
platforms -- this is the correct teardown order everywhere.

diff --git a/src/VideoWorker.cpp b/src/VideoWorker.cpp
--- a/src/VideoWorker.cpp
+++ b/src/VideoWorker.cpp
@@ -830,15 +830,21 @@
   ret = IMP_Encoder_StopRecvPic(encChn);
   LOG_DEBUG_OR_ERROR(ret, "IMP_Encoder_StopRecvPic(" << encChn << ")");

-  if (global_video[encChn]->imp_framesource) {
-    global_video[encChn]->imp_framesource->disable();
-
-    if (global_video[encChn]->imp_encoder) {
-      global_video[encChn]->imp_encoder->deinit();
-      delete global_video[encChn]->imp_encoder;
-      global_video[encChn]->imp_encoder = nullptr;
-    }
-  }
+  // T23/VPU teardown order: detach + destroy the encoder BEFORE disabling the
+  // framesource. IMP_FrameSource_DisableChn on a framesource still bound (via
+  // IMP_System_Bind) to a stopped encoder blocks ~6s waiting for a consumer that
+  // cannot drain, wedging the T23 VPU on an in-process rebuild
+  // (global_restart_video). deinit() unbinds fs->osd->enc and destroys the
+  // channel; only then is the framesource free to disable cleanly.
+  if (global_video[encChn]->imp_encoder) {
+    global_video[encChn]->imp_encoder->deinit();
+    delete global_video[encChn]->imp_encoder;
+    global_video[encChn]->imp_encoder = nullptr;
+  }
+
+  if (global_video[encChn]->imp_framesource) {
+    global_video[encChn]->imp_framesource->disable();
+  }

   {
     std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(global_video[encChn]->privacy_mutex);
